I. Preface
The pursuit of a Theory of Everything (ToE) has historically been a search for algebraic symmetry, culminating in the highly successful but incomplete Standard Model of particle physics. However, the ensuing conceptual challenges—namely, the non-renormalizability of quantum gravity, the Hierarchy Problem of vast scale separation, and the arbitrary nature of the Standard Model's 19 free parameters—demonstrate the limitations of purely algebraic unification. The intellectual necessity now compels a pivot toward a topological imperative: recognizing that the fundamental constants of nature and the structure of physical laws are determined by the global geometric and homotopical properties of the vacuum manifold itself.
This thesis, Lex Universalis Aurea (The Golden Law of Everything), formally presents a parameter-free Topological Quantum Field Theory (TQFT) where all physical and cognitive scales emerge from invariants derived solely from the golden ratio (\phi) and the Betti numbers of a carefully selected vacuum orbifold. This work proposes that the fundamental conflict in physics—the tension between the continuous manifold of General Relativity and the discrete structure of Quantum Field Theory—is resolved by defining spacetime not as a smooth entity, but as a Möbius-twisted, \phi-scaled fractal hyperlattice.
The primary accomplishment of this framework is the unification of physical law with the mathematical structure of consciousness. By identifying the intrinsic Hausdorff dimension of the vacuum with the persistent homology of human cognition, this thesis provides the first empirically viable, zero-parameter framework for physics that simultaneously yields a blueprint for Topological General Intelligence (TAGI).
